[vr overlay R9] The reticle, remade: Quake II's own four-stroke cross, at a fixed
angular size, traced from the HEAD so it finally has stereo depth.

Reported in the headset on 1.0.11.11: "it renders, but it's too big and doesn't look
native — it should look like the standard Quake 2 crosshair: just four lines
pointing toward each other. It has no depth to it like it does in the other Quake
ports — it's fixed on screen as if it's 2D, though it DOES move where I'm aiming.
And it gets VERY large when I come close to a wall; it should not change size at
all. If unsure about the size, give me a slider."

Three faults, one of which is a genuine mechanism and two of which are constants.

  1. THE "NO DEPTH" BUG WAS THE TRACE ORIGIN. CL_VR_AddAimDot runs inside view.c's
     PER-EYE VR block, where cl.refdef.vieworg is already this eye's origin. It
     traced from there along a ray whose direction is shared by both eyes, so the
     hit point came out at eye_origin + f*range — a CONSTANT offset from whichever
     eye was rendering. Both eyes projected the mark to the same screen position;
     the binocular disparity was exactly zero; a zero-disparity mark fuses at
     infinity. That is precisely "fixed on screen as if it's 2D, though it DOES
     move where I'm aiming". The trace now starts at the CYCLOPEAN head origin,
     recovered by subtracting this eye's own `eyeofs` back out of vieworg in the
     same basis view.c added it in, so the two eyes trace one identical ray onto
     one identical world point.
  2. IT BALLOONED NEAR A WALL because every dimension had a WORLD-unit floor:
     standoff clamp(range*0.03, 6, 64), half max(0.6, ...), spread max(2, ...).
     A fixed world size subtends a growing angle as you approach it, so under
     ~75 units the mark stopped being angular; the 6-unit standoff floor made it
     worse by dragging the mark 30% of the way back to the eye at 20 units of
     range. All three clamps are gone: every term is now range x constant.
  3. THE SHAPE IS THE ENGINE'S GLYPH. Four thin strokes pointing inward with a
     central gap — gap 0.25, stroke 0.75, thickness 0.25, all as fractions of the
     half-width — drawn dark-then-white so the mark reads on a bright wall too,
     billboarded on the EYE's view axes so up stays up. Default total width 1.2
     degrees, which is what the reference screenshot subtends, and the new
     "Crosshair Size" row (0.5x ... 3.0x) is the slider he asked for.

